      "text": "To find \\(\\tan V\\) in the right triangle \\(VWX\\) where \\(VX = \\sqrt{13}\\) and \\(VW = 3\\), we need to determine the length of the hypotenuse \\(WX\\). We can use the Pythagorean theorem for this purpose:\n\n\\[WX^2 = VX^2 + VW^2\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[WX^2 = (\\sqrt{13})^2 + 3^2\\]\n\\[WX^2 = 13 + 9\\]\n\\[WX^2 = 22\\]\n\\[WX = \\sqrt{22}\\]\n\nNow, we can find \\(\\tan V\\). In a right triangle, \\(\\tan\\) of an angle is the ratio of the length of the opposite side to the length of the adjacent side. Here, the side opposite to angle \\(V\\) is \\(WV\\) and the side adjacent to angle \\(V\\) is \\(WX\\). So,\n\n\\[\\tan V = \\frac{WV}{WX}\\]\n\nWe already know \\(WV = 3\\) and \\(WX = \\sqrt{22}\\), so:\n\n\\[\\tan V = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}}\\]\n\nTo rationalize the denominator, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by \\(\\sqrt{22}\\):\n\n\\[\\tan V = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{\\sqrt{22}} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{22}}{22}\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\tan V\\) is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{3\\sqrt{22}}{22}}\\]",
